    "icon": "data:image/png;base64,",
    "description": "Tests whether amounts follow Benford's law (first, first-two or second digit), overall or per group, with chi-square, MAD conformity and flagged records for follow-up.",
    "category": "Analytics",
    "subcategory": "Audit",
    "tags": [
        "benford",
        "fraud",
        "audit",
        "forensic",
        "anomaly",
        "digits",
        "data quality",
        "visokio-agents"
    ],
    "introductoryText": "In many real-world sets of amounts (invoices, expenses, payments, claims, populations) about 30% of values start with 1 and under 5% with 9: **Benford's law**. Invented or manipulated figures usually do not follow it, so a deviation is a well-established *screening* signal for auditors and fraud teams. It is not proof: some data legitimately does not follow Benford (prices set by policy, capped amounts, assigned numbers).\n\n- **First digit** test (1 to 9), **first two digits** (10 to 99, sharper, needs more data) or **second digit** (0 to 9)\n- Overall or **per group**: department, supplier, employee, cost centre\n- Per digit: observed vs expected share and a z-statistic (with continuity correction); over- and under-represented digits flagged\n- Per group: **chi-square** and p-value, **mean absolute deviation (MAD)** with Nigrini's conformity bands (*Close*, *Acceptable*, *Marginally acceptable*, *Nonconformity*). Because MAD bands assume large samples, a group is only called *Nonconformity* when the chi-square test is also significant; the expected MAD for a conforming group of that size is shown for comparison\n- The **records** behind the over-represented digits of nonconforming groups, ready for follow-up\n\nAmounts below a minimum (default 10) are ignored, as is usual for first-digit tests; negative amounts are tested on their absolute value. Pure Python: no packages needed.",
    "dependencies": "",
